---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import MarkdownToDocsTool from '../../components/tools/MarkdownToDocsTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { parseMarkdown, renderHtml, summarize, SAMPLE_MARKDOWN } from '../../lib/markdown';

const tool = toolBySlug('markdown-to-google-docs');

// Every example below is produced by the real engine at build time.
const h = (md: string) => renderHtml(parseMarkdown(md));
const sampleBlocks = parseMarkdown(SAMPLE_MARKDOWN);
const stats = summarize(sampleBlocks);
const sampleHtml = renderHtml(sampleBlocks);
const sampleHead = sampleHtml.split('\n').slice(0, 2).join('\n');
const tableTag = sampleHtml.match(/<table[^>]*>/)?.[0] ?? '';
const preTag = sampleHtml.match(/<pre[^>]*>/)?.[0] ?? '';
const codeTag = sampleHtml.match(/<code[^>]*>/)?.[0] ?? '';
if (!tableTag || !preTag || !codeTag) throw new Error('page quotes the table/pre/code tags of the sample');

const nested = h('- a\n  - b\n    1. c');
const tasks = h('- [x] done\n- [ ] todo');
const unsafe = h('[click](javascript:alert(1)) and [ok](https://example.com)');
const rawHtml = h('<b>typed</b> &amp; <script>x</script>');
const image = h('![Logo](https://example.com/logo.png)');
const emptyOut = h('  \n\n');
const tight = h('- a\n- b');
const loose = h('- a\n\n- b');
if (emptyOut !== '') throw new Error('page claims empty input gives empty output');
if (!/<a href="https:\/\/example.com">ok<\/a>/.test(unsafe) || /javascript:/.test(unsafe)) throw new Error('page claims javascript: links are dropped');

const steps = [
  'Paste the Markdown into the left box. The preview on the right shows the formatted result as you type — headings, bold, lists, tables, code, links.',
  'Click <strong>Copy for Google Docs</strong> (or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> in the input box). The formatted text and a plain-text copy both go on the clipboard.',
  'Open your document — <strong>Open a new Google Doc ↗</strong> creates one — and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> on a Mac). Word, Gmail, Outlook, Teams and Notion accept the same paste.',
  'Need the code instead? <strong>Copy HTML</strong> or <strong>Download .html</strong> give you the fragment the preview is made of.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why does pasting Markdown into Google Docs leave the asterisks and hashes in?',
    a: 'A normal paste carries plain text, and Google Docs shows plain text as typed: **bold** stays four characters, not a bold word. Formatting only travels when the clipboard also holds an HTML version of the text, which is what this tool puts there. Docs then reads the HTML and applies its own styles: Heading 1–6, bold, italic, lists, a real table, links.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Google Docs has a “Paste from Markdown” option — why use this?',
    a: 'It does, since July 2024: Tools → Preferences → Enable Markdown, then right-click → Paste from Markdown. If it is on for your account, use it. This page is for when it is not (a Workspace admin has not enabled it, or the destination is Word, Gmail, Outlook, Teams or Notion rather than Docs), when you want to see the result before it lands in the document, or when you need the HTML itself.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do tables and code blocks survive the paste?',
    a: 'Tables paste as real tables: the HTML carries a bordered table with a header row, and Google Docs, Word and Outlook build one from it. Code pastes as monospace text — Docs has no "code block" style, so the tool marks code with a monospace font (Consolas / Courier New) that the editor keeps. Nested lists paste as nested lists; task items paste with ☑ and ☐ characters, since checkboxes are not something HTML can carry into Docs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I download a .docx file?',
    a: 'Not from this page. Paste into Google Docs and use File → Download → Microsoft Word, or paste into Word directly. For a command-line route, pandoc converts a Markdown file to .docx in one step (see the code section below). Download .html gives you a standalone file that Word also opens.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is my document uploaded anywhere?',
    a: 'No. The Markdown is parsed and rendered by JavaScript in this page; the clipboard write happens in your browser. There is no server, so nothing you paste can leave your device, and the only network request on the page is for the page itself.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <MarkdownToDocsTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How formatting travels through the clipboard</h2>
    <p>
      When you copy text from a web page and paste it into Google Docs, the headings and bold come with it. When you
      copy Markdown from a chat window and paste it, they do not. The difference is not the editor, it is the clipboard:
      the first copy carried an HTML flavour alongside the plain text; the second carried only text, and Markdown's <code>**</code> and <code>#</code> are just characters to Docs. Docs' own <em>Paste from Markdown</em> option
      exists for exactly this and is worth switching on if your account offers it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tool converts the Markdown to HTML in your browser, shows you that HTML rendered, and on <strong>Copy for Google Docs</strong> writes both the HTML and a plain-text version to the clipboard. Any editor
      that understands formatted paste — Google Docs, Microsoft Word, Gmail, Outlook, Teams, Notion, Confluence — reads
      the HTML and applies its native styles. A plain text box, a terminal or a code editor takes the text version. The
      HTML is deliberately simple: semantic tags, a monospace font on code, a border on tables, nothing else, so the
      destination's own styles apply rather than fighting inline ones.
    </p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Load sample</strong> inserts a deploy checklist with {stats.headings} heading, {stats.lists} lists (one nested,
      one of tasks), {stats.tables} table, {stats.codeBlocks} code block and {stats.links} links. The preview shows it formatted;
      the HTML that goes on the clipboard begins:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{sampleHead}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Pasted into Google Docs, that is a Heading 2, a paragraph with a bold phrase, monospace <code>npm test</code> and an
      italic phrase, then a numbered list with a nested bulleted list, a three-column table with a header row, an indented
      quote, a monospace command and a two-item list beginning with ☑ and ☐. The table is <code>{tableTag}</code> and the code block is <code>{preTag}</code>, which is what makes Docs keep the border and
      the font.
    </p>

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Copy for Google Docs</strong> puts the formatted result on the clipboard as HTML plus plain text. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> in the input box does the same. If your browser cannot write HTML to the clipboard directly (older Firefox), the tool selects the preview and copies that instead, which carries the same formatting.</li>
      <li><strong>Open a new Google Doc ↗</strong> opens <code>docs.new</code> in a new tab — a blank document ready for the paste. It is a plain link; nothing is sent to it.</li>
      <li><strong>Copy HTML</strong> copies the HTML fragment as text, for a CMS field, an email template or a page.</li>
      <li><strong>Download .html</strong> saves the fragment wrapped in a minimal page (<code>&lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;</code> and a title) as <code>document.html</code>, which Word opens directly.</li>
      <li><strong>Load sample</strong> inserts the checklist shown above; <strong>Clear</strong> empties the input and the preview.</li>
      <li>The <strong>preview</strong> is the exact fragment the clipboard gets, styled only enough to read. Its scrollbar and resize handle are the page's; they are not part of the copy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nested lists</strong> nest in the HTML, so they nest in Docs: <code>- a / (two spaces) - b / (four) 1. c</code> gives <code>{nested}</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Tight and loose lists</strong> follow Markdown: items with no blank line between them paste as plain list items (<code>{tight}</code>); items separated by blank lines carry a paragraph each (<code>{loose}</code>), which Docs shows with extra spacing.</li>
      <li><strong>Task lists</strong> become <code>{tasks}</code> — a character, not a checkbox, because HTML on the clipboard cannot create Docs checkboxes.</li>
      <li><strong>Code keeps its characters</strong> and gets the monospace font through <code>{codeTag}</code>; the language name after the opening fence is not carried over. Syntax colouring is not applied — Docs has nowhere to keep it.</li>
      <li><strong>Images</strong> are passed as their address: <code>{image}</code>. Whether the destination fetches and shows the picture is up to that application; Docs generally does for a public URL, an email draft may not.</li>
      <li><strong>Links with a <code>javascript:</code> or <code>data:</code> address are dropped</strong>, keeping only their text, so a pasted document can never carry a script: <code>{unsafe}</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>HTML typed into the Markdown is text, not markup.</strong> <code>&lt;b&gt;typed&lt;/b&gt;</code> is escaped and shows as those characters: <code>{rawHtml}</code>. If you have HTML already, paste it into the destination directly.</li>
      <li><strong>Headings map to Heading 1–6</strong> in Docs, from <code>#</code> through <code>######</code>; a <code>#######</code> with seven hashes is a paragraph, as in Markdown.</li>
      <li><strong>The clipboard needs a user action.</strong> Browsers only allow a clipboard write from a click or a key press, which is why there is no automatic copy on paste. If a browser extension blocks clipboard access, select the preview by hand and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>: the selection carries the formatting too.</li>
      <li><strong>Empty input</strong> shows the empty preview and disables the buttons. Half a megabyte of Markdown renders in well under a second; typing is debounced so keystrokes never wait on it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same thing in code</h2>
    <h3>Shell (pandoc)</h3>
    <pre><code>pandoc -f gfm -t html notes.md -o notes.html    # the same kind of fragment
pandoc -f gfm -o notes.docx notes.md              # straight to Word</code></pre>
    <h3>Python</h3>
    <pre><code>{`import markdown
html = markdown.markdown(text, extensions=["tables", "fenced_code"])`}</code></pre>
    <h3>JavaScript (browser)</h3>
    <pre><code>{`import { marked } from "marked";
const html = marked.parse(text);
await navigator.clipboard.write([
  new ClipboardItem({
    "text/html": new Blob([html], { type: "text/html" }),
    "text/plain": new Blob([text], { type: "text/plain" }),
  }),
]);`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      When the destination is a chat box rather than a document, formatting is the problem, not the goal: <a href="/tools/markdown-to-plain-text">Markdown to Plain Text</a> removes it while keeping the structure, or
      rewrites it as Slack, WhatsApp or Google Chat markup. Text that came out of an AI assistant with curly quotes and
      em dashes can go through the <a href="/tools/ai-text-cleaner">AI Text Cleaner</a> first.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
